Has anyone written an algorithms or architecture paper on R-INLA?

I am new to R-INLA. I have read the paper by Rue, Martino, and Chopin (2009) and the paper by Martins, Simpson, Lindgren, and Rue (2013). I am wanting to learn the software architecture and what algorithms are used in both R-INLA and the underlying GMRFlib. I get the gist of the method from the papers but I am at a disadvantage, being a computer scientist, in understanding the details of the algorithms and their implementation. I suspect I will have to read the source code and reverse engineer the architecture.

I am interested in building a Bayesian model solver for big data sets. To make this concrete, let's start simple with Bayesian linear regression with a data set that has ten billion rows and a hundred real valuated covariates and ten thousand sparse indicator covariates. I just made up this example to given an example that will never fit in R. I am interested in studying INLA to see if it can be solved with the Map-Reduce paradigm use in Spark, an open source distributed computing system. Two areas where there are known parallel methods with Map-Reduce algorithms for big data sets are numerical linear algebra and convex optimization. I am interested in learning how INLA uses linear algebra and if the various optimization steps are convex optimization problems. One area that has been very useful for big data are randomized numerical linear algebra algorithms. These algorithms can invert a nxm matrix in O(n x m x log(m)). Being yet another approximation in the algorithms, it is unknown how the approximation error would propagate through the algorithms.

What I would like to build is an open source Bayesian solver to contribute to the MLIB package in the Spark distribution. I am happy to correspond with anyone who is interested.

